WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.440
A surprising hostage situation,&nbsp;
a visually stunning final course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.880
and one chef's legacy literally going&nbsp;
up in smoke. The ending of "The Menu"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.700
continues to delight viewers for so many reasons.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:17.160
One of the biggest mysteries in "The Menu"&nbsp;
is Chef Slowiksince it seems like he's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:21.900
going through a full-on breakdown throughout the&nbsp;
night. He makes a creepy early impression with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.520
a disturbing tale of childhood trauma, made all&nbsp;
the more chilling by Ralph Fiennes's incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.020
performance. Plus, he's somehow been able to&nbsp;
convince his staff to follow him to the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.520
literally. He's ready to end it all, and&nbsp;
he's planning to take his guests, his staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.320
and himself out in a ball of fire. So, why is&nbsp;
Chef Slowik suddenly ready to end everything?

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.880
The accomplished chef explains that after&nbsp;
years of cooking overly artsy food for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.020
unworthy and disrespectful clients, he's&nbsp;
completely lost his passion for cooking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.560
He now looks to end his suffering&nbsp;
by dishing out some cold revenge.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.940
Chef Slowik's latest menu is simply him&nbsp;
punishing those that have brought him into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.320
this life and embody everything he's grown&nbsp;
to hate about the avant-garde food world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.240
including petty food critics, narcissistic&nbsp;
businessmen, and pretentious foodies. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.640
growing disdain perfectly explains Chef Slowik's&nbsp;
generally cold presence. In fact, his existence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:16.140
has become so bad that he would rather die&nbsp;
than have to cook another meal ever again.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:20.640
“I’ve been fooled into trying to satisfy&nbsp;
people who can never be satisfied.”

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.020
Chef Slowik gradually reveals&nbsp;
why he is ready to end it all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.840
and get some cold revenge on his guests. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.340
it's more difficult to figure out why his staff&nbsp;
is so willing to follow him into the abyss.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.180
Most of them are young and could have some&nbsp;
promising cooking careers ahead of them thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:41.040
to the impeccable dishes they serve. So, why are&nbsp;
they willing to go down in flames and never even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.660
think about fighting back? Sadly, the answer&nbsp;
is that there's no place they'd rather be.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.800
It would be easy to say that Chef Slowik&nbsp;
simply brainwashes the cooks to follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:54.000
his order because of how in sync they&nbsp;
are with him at every turn. Truthfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.960
everyone's more than willing to be there&nbsp;
just so they can work for Chef Slowik.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.280
Slowik's staff represents the allure&nbsp;
of desperately wanting to make it in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.480
tough industry and the willingness to do&nbsp;
just about anything to attain accolades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.260
and respect. They might know that this&nbsp;
will be the last meal they cook, but if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.580
they're working on Chef Slowik's masterpiece&nbsp;
then it's an opportunity they can't pass up.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.480
It's basically the same reason that&nbsp;
people don't quit after Gordon Ramsay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.840
screams and yells at them during&nbsp;
service. Their staying mentality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:27.060
embodies their views of their art being more&nbsp;
valued alongside Chef Slowik, even in death.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.560
Throughout the film, there's a silver door&nbsp;
in the restaurant that catches the attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:36.420
of Anya Taylor-Joy's Margot, which adds&nbsp;
another mystery to the whole ordeal. Once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.020
Margot is able to go into Chef Slowik's&nbsp;
house while running an errand for him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.021
she discovers what's behind an&nbsp;
identical door in his house.

00:02:43.021 --> 00:02:47.100
What she finds could be easily considered&nbsp;
Chef Slowik's planning and motivation room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.220
since there are newspaper clippings&nbsp;
on the walls of some of his reviews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.800
and photos that embody his rise to prominence.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.880
However, Margot makes a more important&nbsp;
discovery that inspires her fight for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.800
survival. In his secret room, she sees a&nbsp;
photo of Chef Slowik working as a chef at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.180
local burger joint. He's smiling, and it looks&nbsp;
as though he truly has a passion for cooking.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:09.900
It might look unimportant to most,&nbsp;
but Margot sees it as a symbol of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.280
Chef Slowik's love for food and a reminder&nbsp;
of the simpler life he had before becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.300
a world-famous culinary auteur. As&nbsp;
well as giving Margot the opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.500
to survive Chef Slowik's final meal,&nbsp;
it delves deep into the chef's psyche.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.040
“Yes, what?”

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.220
“Yes chef.”

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.700
Chef Slowik tells Margot that&nbsp;
his guests are being punished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:33.240
for destroying his love of food. He&nbsp;
reveals that they also embody a world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:37.080
of cooking that's devoid of any real&nbsp;
passion, but he views her as different.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:41.220
Unlike the other guests, he respects her&nbsp;
and believes that she is much more real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:45.780
than anyone else sitting in the dining room.&nbsp;
Despite this respect, Chef Slowik doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.800
plan on letting Margot go, so she's forced&nbsp;
to figure out a way to leave on her own.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:54.720
Inspired by the photo of Chef Slowik cooking&nbsp;
burgers in his youth, Margot challenges him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.580
to make her a simple cheeseburger and fries. In&nbsp;
this moment, there's a legitimate power shift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.540
and Margot even does Chef Slowik's signature&nbsp;
hand clap to get everyone's attention.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:08.400
With confidence, she looks totally in&nbsp;
control as she's served a mouth-watering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.880
burger with fries. By touching on&nbsp;
the chef's long-buried desire to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:15.780
make simple food with love, Margot finds&nbsp;
a way to escape from his revenge menu.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:20.940
Margot's discovery of Chef Slowik's past&nbsp;
as a burger chef is a major turning point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:25.800
in the film and heavily inspires her to order a&nbsp;
cheeseburger in order to possibly save herself.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:29.340
Her request is granted, and Chef&nbsp;
Slowik makes one hell of a plate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:32.520
of cheeseburger and fries that makes&nbsp;
you want to rip it off the screen and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.700
eat it yourself. But there's more to&nbsp;
this cheeseburger than meets the eye.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:39.480
“I’ll make you feel as though you’re eating&nbsp;
the first cheeseburger you ever ate.”

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:42.000
Not only does this cheeseburger connect to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.720
former part of Chef Slowik's life as&nbsp;
a chef who genuinely loved to cook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:49.920
but it symbolizes a part of cooking that's&nbsp;
lost on most of the people in the room.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:55.740
It represents that brand of easy cooking that's&nbsp;
timeless and never needs to be flashy in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:00.900
establish a connection with people. It's innocent&nbsp;
and has real flavor to it, not because of special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:05.520
spices but because the love and touch of the&nbsp;
person cooking it is present in every bite.

00:05:05.701 --> 00:05:09.481
“When I eat your food, it tastes&nbsp;
like it was made with no love.”

00:05:09.481 --> 00:05:13.500
Chef Slowik doesn't even prepare a&nbsp;
fancy cheeseburger and fries. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:17.940
it's just a nice simple burger sitting on&nbsp;
top of a paper plate that easily puts a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:21.600
smile on a person's face. It certainly&nbsp;
puts a smile on Chef Slowik's face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.500
and reignites his radiating love of&nbsp;
food that had been gone for so long.

00:05:25.621 --> 00:05:31.081
“Now that is a cheeseburger.”

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:36.300
After getting Chef Slowik to fill her request for&nbsp;
a cheeseburger, there's a tense moment in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:40.920
everyone wonders if he'll actually let Margot go.&nbsp;
Even in her request to have the rest of the meal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:45.780
to go, viewers can't help but question if her ruse&nbsp;
will actually work, but surprisingly, it does.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:50.220
Chef Slowik wraps up all her food in a&nbsp;
paper bag, gives her a party favor bag,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.580
and tells his staff to let her walk&nbsp;
out the door and leave. But why does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.860
he have a change of heart when it comes to Margot?

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:00.900
“This menu, this guest list, this entire&nbsp;
evening has been painstakingly planned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:03.401
and you were not a part of that plan.”

00:06:03.401 --> 00:06:07.500
Throughout "The Menu," Chef Slowik constantly&nbsp;
tells Margot that she's different from the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:12.180
guests because she seems more genuine and real,&nbsp;
and isn't meant to be there in the first place.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:15.840
By ordering a cheeseburger, Margot&nbsp;
proves that she's not like anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:19.800
else in the room as she can appreciate&nbsp;
a simple home-cooked meal. Chef Slowik&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.980
likely lets her go because she doesn't&nbsp;
deserve to die with the rest of the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:27.182
or because he respects how clever she&nbsp;
is when she makes that to-go request.

00:06:27.182 --> 00:06:30.180
Either way, Margot uses Chef&nbsp;
Slowik's original love of food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:33.780
to make a last-ditch effort to&nbsp;
survive, and it totally works.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:38.220
Once Margot is allowed to leave,&nbsp;
everyone wonders if Chef Slowik&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:41.220
will have a change of heart and end the&nbsp;
bloodshed that's consumed the night.

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:46.020
After all, Chef Slowik has been reminded of his&nbsp;
love of food and cooked a meal that brings some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:50.760
genuine warmth to the character for the first&nbsp;
time in the film. However, once Margot leaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:55.320
he reverts back to his old form as the&nbsp;
final course is ready to be served. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:58.920
why doesn't Margot's cheeseburger&nbsp;
request change anything for Chef Slowik?

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:03.360
Chef Slowik doesn't have a real change of&nbsp;
heart at any point, and he's still committed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.500
to his final menu. Just because Margot showed&nbsp;
that she isn't like the others in the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:12.060
doesn't mean that it changes anything for&nbsp;
any of the other diners, or Chef Slowik.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:15.900
He always felt that Margot was different&nbsp;
and once she's out of the line of fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:19.440
there's nothing stopping him from&nbsp;
completing his masterpiece. Frankly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.520
even the other guests seem to accept&nbsp;
their fate, as no one tries to stop Chef&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:28.620
Slowik in the final moments. In fact, Judith&nbsp;
Light's Anne even waves for Margot to leave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:32.760
showing that the remaining guests&nbsp;
are already resigned to their fate.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:37.080
Although Margot is able to escape, the&nbsp;
other guests are basically trapped for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:40.620
the final course. Chef Slowik suggests&nbsp;
that the diners could fight back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:44.160
and he even allows the men to run and&nbsp;
hide on the island during the "Men's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:48.840
Folly" course. But in all seriousness, could&nbsp;
anyone else actually escape if they tried?

00:07:49.181 --> 00:07:53.501
“To our male diners, we now&nbsp;
offer you the chance to escape.”

00:07:53.501 --> 00:07:56.820
The answer is likely no. The&nbsp;
Hawthorne staff are plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:59.700
fit to fight off any physical&nbsp;
advances made by the guests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:04.020
and no one is able to effectively hide during&nbsp;
their terrifying rendition of hide-and-seek.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:08.700
Even if other diners tried to play on Chef&nbsp;
Slowik's humanity in the way that Margot did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:12.720
he would probably see their attempts as&nbsp;
insincere and keep them captive anyway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:15.960
Throughout the film, the guests are&nbsp;
told there's no escape for them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:18.600
and that seems like the case&nbsp;
right up to the bitter end.

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:21.660
After all of the many horrors&nbsp;
seen throughout the night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:25.740
everyone's left wondering what Chef Slowik&nbsp;
has up his sleeve for the final course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:29.220
especially as it's likely going&nbsp;
to be the last meal anyone has.

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:32.700
Like any classic auteur chef,&nbsp;
he can't do something simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:37.860
and goes for an avant-garde take on s'mores.&nbsp;
As for why he chooses this particular dish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:42.120
Chef Slowik talks about s'mores being&nbsp;
the staple of any innocent childhood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:45.720
curling up around the fire with family&nbsp;
and friends roasting marshmallows.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:49.740
This final course seemingly represents&nbsp;
the innocence that was lost by Chef&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:53.220
Slowik himself throughout his career.&nbsp;
Instead of roasting marshmallows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:55.980
the plan is to roast everyone inside Hawthorne.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:00.660
It's a visually stunning dish, prepared in&nbsp;
a typically theatrical fashion. Everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:04.200
and everyone going up in flames&nbsp;
is a horrifying sight to behold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:07.800
especially as all of the guests&nbsp;
are covered in marshmallows. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.060
a fiery final course with some solid&nbsp;
symbolism and horrifying conclusions.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:18.240
With Chef Slowik's menu finally coming together&nbsp;
and his final course in action, there's only one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:23.700
way for things to end, and that's in flames.&nbsp;
Just after saying his goodbyes and giving his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:28.800
final words to the guests, Chef Slowik lights&nbsp;
the room ablaze and puts an end to this night of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:34.200
horrors. Meanwhile, Margot's escape is not quite&nbsp;
going to plan since the boat breaks down on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:39.960
water. From her POV, we see the final results of&nbsp;
Chef Slowik's menu and what's left of Hawthorne.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:44.760
As Margot looks back at Hawthorne, she&nbsp;
sees flames bursting out of windows and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.400
the entire building starting&nbsp;
to burn down. The restaurant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:52.260
and possibly the entire island, will simply&nbsp;
turn to ash before anyone can save it.

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:57.360
Chef Slowik's vision has been fully realized.&nbsp;
The final course also presents the perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:02.700
backdrop for Margot to simply keep eating her&nbsp;
cheeseburger in peace, knowing that she survived.

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:06.540
While a sequel to "The Menu" hasn't been&nbsp;
announced, fans are wondering whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:09.960
Chef Slowick might return to torment&nbsp;
a new set of guests in the future.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:15.120
However, the movie ends pretty definitively, with&nbsp;
the completion of the chef's menu literally going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:20.100
up in smoke. As Chef Slowick is last seen inside&nbsp;
the restaurant with the guests and his staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:24.480
it seems unlikely that he has any plans to&nbsp;
escape the fiery mess of his own making.

00:10:24.634 --> 00:10:29.974
“You represent the ruin of my art and my life.”

00:10:29.974 --> 00:10:34.380
At the end of "The Menu," Hawthorne is&nbsp;
burned to the ground. Margot is the sole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:38.280
survivor. Chef Slowik's legacy is&nbsp;
destroyed by his own masterpiece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:44.160
with no real successor mentioned or hinted at.&nbsp;
As a result, it seems likely that "The Menu" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:49.680
a superb one-off horror story full of decadent&nbsp;
food, sharp comedy, and disturbing moments.

